  "query_preview": "..(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 335 AM EST THU NOV 26 2026\n\nThe extended period opens Friday evening with the area located on\nthe backside of a departing upper-level low. Showers are expected\nto taper off from west to east in the late evening before ceasing\novernight. A rain-snow mix remains possible in the high terrain of\nsoutheast Kentucky near the Virginia border, though warmer trends\nin recent guidance have pared back the more widespread wintry mix\nadvertised in earlier forecast packages. Flow aloft looks to be\nmore zonal than northwesterly, and with model 850 mb temperatures\nwarming to around 3-4 C, subfreezing surface temperatures seem\nless likely by 12Z Saturday morning. Previous shifts noted a cold\nbias within the NBM and had blended some CONS data with the NBM to\nyield a slightly warmer solution. These edits were kept, with\ntemperatures expected to cool into the low to mid 30s for most of\nthe area.\n\nBrief upper-level height rises and surface high pressure build into\nthe region on Saturday, allowing temperatures to rebound into the\nmid 50s under fair skies. However, the next system approaches\nquickly as an upstream shortwave ejects out of the Rockies and digs\nacross the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes through Sunday. Southerly\nflow ahead of an approaching surface cold front will advect milder\nair into eastern Kentucky on Sunday, pushing afternoon highs into\nthe upper 50s to low 60s before rain chances return along and\nahead of the frontal passage. Winds will also turn breezy Sunday\nafternoon and evening as the front moves through. The 12Z LREF\nGrand Ensemble shows a general 40-50% chance of wind gusts\nreaching at least 30 mph south of Jackson Sunday evening, with the\nhighest probabilities of 60-75% over the high terrain of Harlan\nCounty.\n\nHigh pressure builds back across the Ohio Valley behind the front\non Monday and settles over the central Appalachians by Tuesday,\nbringing a stretch of dry weather through much of the new workweek.\nTemperatures will moderate Monday through Wednesday, with highs\nwarming from the low 50s to near 60 by midweek.\n\nMultiple models are resolving another upper-level low off the\nCalifornia coast Tuesday. By Wednesday, this low will dig into the\nFour Corners region before ejecting east across the Central Plains\non Thursday. This system could bring the next round of\nprecipitation to the area later Thursday and onward.",
